\title{pneumoalloc: serotype-specific pneumococcal carriage with multiple
serotype carriage}
\description{
Bayesian models for repeated cross-sectional carriage surveys in which
a child can carry more than one serotype. Every model standardises its
estimates over the observed covariate distribution of the study
population (posterior g-computation), and prevalence ratios are formed
within each posterior draw.
}
\details{
\strong{Step 1: overall and multiple serotype carriage}
\itemize{
\item \code{\link[=fit_carriage_states]{fit_carriage_states()}}: multinomial logit for the number of
detected serotypes (none, one, two, three, ...), giving overall
carriage, multiple serotype carriage and each carriage state;
\item \code{\link[=fit_multiple_types]{fit_multiple_types()}}: among children with multiple carriage,
multinomial logit for VT-only, NVT-only, mixed VT-dominant and mixed
NVT-dominant carriage.
}

\strong{Step 2: serotype-specific carriage (unified framework)}

\code{\link[=fit_carriage]{fit_carriage()}} fits one joint model of overall carriage (logistic),
serotype composition among carriers (fractional multinomial logit) and
density among carriers (lognormal with a multiplicity sub-model), in
all children (\code{population = "all"}) or among children with multiple
carriage (\code{population = "multiple"}). The four allocation approaches
differ \strong{only} in the allocation weights of the composition
component:\tabular{llll}{
   approach \tab name \tab allocation of a carriage episode \tab carrier weight \cr
   i \tab \code{dominant} \tab all to the dominant serotype \tab 1 \cr
   ii \tab \code{equal} \tab 1 / n to each of the n serotypes \tab 1 \cr
   iii \tab \code{abundance} \tab relative abundance of each serotype \tab 1 \cr
   iv \tab \code{density} \tab relative abundance of each serotype \tab \eqn{\Theta_i / \bar{\Theta}_t} \cr
}


Here \eqn{\Theta_i} is the total pneumococcal density of child i,
\eqn{\theta_{is}} the relative abundance of serotype s (so the
serotype-specific density is \eqn{\theta_{is} \Theta_i}), and
\eqn{\bar{\Theta}_t} the mean total density among the carriers of the
same survey round t. Normalising within each round keeps the weights of a
round summing to its number of carriers.

